mer of 1989 you were 21  back then what kind of change did you  want to see  well the political reform was promised  by chinese region by deng xiaoping to  chinese people  we were simply demanding they fulfill  their promises  but in china and even in 1989  10 years after the dung shopping era of  reopening reform  still it is a very very uh suppressed uh  regime a suppressed society taking the  street  uh of beijing is not an easy um  thing to do it's not uh they're they're  very  they're dangerous but i think the  students  in beijing across the city and then  across the country have demonstrated a  strong  uh determination that we want that  political reform  once we were promised and then their  response from the government at the  beginning was like police batons and  then  uh a fake dialogue that they they  struck a dialogue to uh uh to uh  uh uh to have the government organize  the student organizat  organizations members to have them so we  escalated  the movement into hunger strike we have  get the support  from all over the country all over the  world  but we could not move the emperor in who  lives in the  i mean we call themselves the general  secretary so uh they answered us at the  end  with bloodshed uh later known to the  world as  june 4th massacre absolutely and i mean  it was a massacre that shocked the world  indeed  and i'm wondering what what you're  thinking was back then i mean did you  suspect  you know as you were going through the  hunger strikes as you were demonstrating  that the chinese government would indeed  crack down so hard and send in tanks and  literally run down the protesters  we never thought that the chinese  government would  actually mobilize standing troops  against peaceful demonstrators  uh it's part of the par chinese  communist party's propaganda  is that they are the people's government  and then  the outstanding army people's liberation  army is people's army  it's unthinkable for any chinese citizen  in 1989 before the june 4th before the  night of june force  to imagine chinese regime open fire  to its own people  [Music]  so it certainly didn't occur to us we  thought okay yeah they're going to  suppress the movement  probably using the police baton and then  uh club us out of the square  uh using real ammunition and then roll  over  uh people protester on this on the  street  with uh you know um tanks and military  uh people movers and that is just  uh it shocked all of us i think you just  said it shocked the world yes  it also certainly shocked uh a billion  chinese  and you had to flee china we understand  after uh the 1989 massacre you you  haven't seen your family since  um when you reflect i mean  this commitment that you have was it  worth the decades of sacrifice that  followed  well uh if you if you ask me is it worth  it i don't know how to how to calculate  it but i can answer  you in a different angle that i don't  regret what i did  we didn't do anything wrong the price we  paid was hot or high  so high we don't know how to uh really  calculate uh  is it worth it but we also know  the what we did in tiananmen inspired  uh people to follow around the world  especially in york  in the country of in germany i mean it  basically leads to  the peaceful ending of the people's uh  uh demonstration in  at that time east germany and then  eventually lead to the uh renew a  falling of the berlin wall and the  reunification of germany  the impact of the 1989 student movement  is huge  to the world unfortunately just not  to china where kaichi we thank you so  much for joining us here on dw news one  of the leaders of the student  democracy movement in china in 1989  we appreciate it it's my pleasure  and as we've been hearing on this 32nd  anniversary police in hong kong have  arrested pro-democracy  activist chao hung-tung usually  she would be leading a mass vigil for  the victims of the tiananmen square  massacre but hong kong authorities  have banned the commemoration for a  second year in a row  dw correspondent phoebe kong met with  her recently  and her report was filmed before the  arrest  a warning shot to would-be protesters on  the anniversary of the day  the quash democracy activist ciao hang  tong  arrested by plainclothes police her  alleged crime  promoting unauthorized assembly  chao was one of the few leaders of hong  kong's pro-democracy movement  not already behind bars the arrest comes  a day after authorities threatened to  jail anyone attending the vigil  for up to five years  for over three decades the sea of  candlelight in hong kong  kept the events of 1989 alive the city's  annual vigil  a symbol of defiance unthinkable on  mainland china  but now even handing out candles is  heavily policed  weeks ahead of the 32nd anniversary the  organization hong kong alliance  led by chao began promoting the annual  commemoration  the first since beijing imposed a  national security law here  ciao at the time eerily pressured  people are even more supportive i would  say but the support is  less like expressive it's actually  gone beyond just the remembrance of  tamman it's also  go to the heart of what  hong kong is whether we still have to  fight  fighting our heart whether we are still  resisting where we can still  preserve our freedom by our own actions  also we'll share this faith with the  people who are already arrested and in  custody  but their hands are largely tied police  have manned the vigil for the second  year in a row  citing cobit's safety restrictions  although other mass gatherings like  concerts and soccer matches  have resumed  just two days before the anniversary the  alliance's june 4 museum  a museum commemorating the events of  1989  was closed down due to a licensing  investigation by the authorities  many of the organization's leading  members are in jail for protesting  including chairman lee chuck yan  we spoke to the veteran activist shortly  before he was sentenced to 18 months in  prison  we are in the darkness we are like in a  tunnel but if everyone still come up  with  a candle that is the spirit that we are  looking for  i believe in the people hong kong we  live in truth make sure  that the hong kong people know that we  never give up  even though maybe we have to go to jail  for the alliance it's not just about  commemorating the protests of 1989  but about being able to dissent to be  able to call for an end to one party  rule  but democracy protests are now labeled  as subversion by beijing  it could be a reason to outlaw the  activism and the alliance itself  we are not sell sending ourselves  there's no question that the government  wants to eradicate  the risk is not insisting on some slogan  or some  some principle the risk is what we are  doing  if you are sincerely fighting for the  end of one party dictatorship there will  always be that risk  there is cannot be avoided by just like  crossing out one of the slogans whether  this happens  sooner or later this happened a long way  away depends on how hard it  resists right workers at the alliance  june 4th museum have begun preserving  the [ __ ] square exhibits in digital  format  if china continues to close down all  avenues of physical protests in hong  kong  moving historical evidence to an online  museum may end up being one of the last  remaining ways  of showing resistance  and for more let's bring in dw hong kong  correspondent  phoebe kong who put that piece together  for us phoebe joins us now phoebe  the arrest of chao hong tang just walk  us through the significance of this move  and  what signal it sends to the  pro-democracy movement in hong kong  well the 37-year-old lawyer  is one of the prominent activists in  hong kong and also the core member of  the annual candlelight visual  organization hong kong alliances  mentioned in the report that she has  been engaging in  chinese human rights work and also local  activism  for long periods of time so her arrest  which is pretty high profile on these  sensitive dates on the anniversary of  the attainment crackdown which is  of like one of the most insensitive  political taboo in china  that uh her arrest actually is a warning  sent by the authorities  to the democracy camp and also the  general public in hong kong as a whole  that the authorities are not going to  tolerate  any kind of commemoration that not only  they are going to ban  the uh signature candlelight vigil but  also going after  individuals who want to sustain that  kind of commemoration and assistance  so um this is uh definitely a deterrent  on these sensitive days that  the authorities want to stand out all  kind of commemoration  or any kind of physical morning that  about this a  very um like uh the throne of  the communist party uh the chinese  ruling party  so the official ban is then in place  talk with us about the mood are people  still determined  to gather  well um the atmosphere on the ground is  really tense like uh  days or even weeks ahead of the  anniversary the local community and  governments  are all like discussing about like  whether  it is still legal to commemorate the  june 4 of tiananmen crackdown in hong  kong in  2021 that and many officials  have been warning the public especially  the law enforcement the police  officers they they're warning the public  that um not to take any risk of  like endangering themselves by putting  themselves in  uh under like um kind of like a possible  offense of the  uh the public ordinance and uh  also the national security law this is  the first anniversary since the  implementation of the national security  law  but of course some activist groups are  still uh  wanting to carry on the commemoration by  maybe setting up some  street booths later on in hong kong  today on the streets  and some churches are holding um like  candlelight vigil  or players gatherings within their  premises but they are all warned by the  offices that the police actually  deployed up to 7 000 offices today  just to guard every corner of hong kong  and especially the now banned kennel  division venue the victoria park  they have already sealed up that park to  really  try to stop anyone to um carry on this  kind of signature activism  uh today but uh we are expecting to see  some kind of defiance and we will see  like  how the police will going to react that  they have already  and intimidate people and threatening  like a rest  of today phoebe kong in hong kong thank  you

